# Key Takeaways

- A vector is a fundamental R data structure.
- A vector normally contains values of the same basic type.
- `c()` is the most common way to create a vector.
- R uses **1-based indexing**.
- Positive indices select elements.
- Negative indices exclude elements.
- Logical indexing is one of the most important techniques in R data analysis.
- `:` and `seq()` create sequences.
- `rep()` repeats values.
- R performs vectorized operations automatically.
- `sort()`, `order()`, `unique()`, `which()`, `any()`, and `all()` are useful vector operations.
- `NA` represents missing data.
- `na.rm = TRUE` can be used to ignore missing values in many functions.
- R automatically performs type coercion when necessary.
- Understanding vectors is essential before learning data frames and `dplyr`.
